seal - turns a directory into one encrypted file, and back
==========================================================

Disk encryption protects everything while the computer is off. Once
it is on and you are logged in, every file is open to whatever runs
as you. seal keeps the files you don't need every day locked even
then: one command turns a directory into one encrypted file, and one
command gives it back.

    seal baba                  baba/ -> baba.seal, then baba/ goes
    seal -o holiday.mp4 baba   any name: baba/ -> holiday.mp4
    unseal baba.seal           -> into RAM: /run/user/UID/seal/baba,
                               and the link baba -> it, next to the file
    seal baba                  (the link) back into baba.seal; the RAM
                               copy and the link go
    seal -a                    everything unsealed back into its file
    seal -l                    what is unsealed in RAM, from where, and
                               its link
    seal -c                    take away links whose directory is gone
    unseal -d . baba.seal      -> ./baba on the disk, baba.seal goes
    unseal -t baba.seal        check it and list what it holds

It asks for the password on the terminal, with nothing echoed: twice
when sealing (a typo would lock the files away for good), once when
unsealing. It says what it did:

    baba -> baba.seal (3 directories, 12 files, 1 link, 40.2 MiB)

Options:

    -o FILE   seal: the file to write (default DIR.seal; for a
              directory from RAM, the file it was unsealed from)
    -d DIR    unseal onto the disk, into DIR, and remove FILE
    -k        keep: seal leaves DIR, unseal -d leaves FILE
    -n        unseal: make no link next to FILE
    -t        unseal: only check FILE and list what is in it
    -f        unseal: go on when FILE is damaged or the password
              seems wrong (see below)
    -s        read the password from standard input (one line), for
              scripts: pass show baba | seal -s baba
    -q        say nothing but errors (and -t's list)
    -l        seal: list the directories unsealed in RAM
    -a        seal: seal all of them back (a password for each)
    -c        seal: take away the links whose directory is gone
    -u        seal -u is unseal

Exit status 0 when all went well, 1 when not (it says why), 2 for
wrong options.


Unsealing into RAM
------------------

By default unseal puts the directory into RAM, never onto the disk:
into $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/seal, /run/user/UID/seal or /dev/shm/seal-UID
(the first that is a tmpfs; only yours, mode 700). The plain files
never touch the disk, and they are gone at shutdown. Work in it as
in any directory, then seal it again: it goes back into the file it
came from, which is replaced only once the new one is written and
checked. Until then the sealed file stays as it was, so a crash or a
reboot loses only what changed since unsealing.

So you can find it where it was, unseal leaves a link next to the
sealed file, named like the directory: unseal baba.seal makes
baba -> /run/user/UID/seal/baba. Work in baba as usual; seal baba
seals the directory behind the link, and takes the link away. seal -a
does it for every unsealed directory at once. (If something named
baba is already there, unseal says so and makes no link; -n makes
none.)

Each link is written down in ~/.system/state/seal/links before it is
made. After a crash or a reboot RAM is empty and the links point
nowhere: seal -c takes them away, and so does every login (~/.profile
runs seal -c -q). So someone who takes the disk finds no plain files,
only, until the next login, links pointing nowhere. The list holds the
links' names while they exist: they are the directories' names, which
the sealed files otherwise hide.

unseal -d puts it onto the disk instead, when it should stay open.


What it does, step by step
--------------------------

seal DIR:
 1. asks for the password twice;
 2. packs DIR into a file next to the output, under a hidden name,
    encrypting as it goes;
 3. reads that file back, decrypts it and compares every name, type,
    mode, link and byte with DIR;
 4. only then gives it its name, and only then removes DIR, and only
    what it sealed: a file that changed or appeared meanwhile is left,
    and named.
If anything goes wrong before step 4, nothing has changed.

unseal FILE unpacks into a hidden directory first and gives it its
name at the end. A wrong password, or a file that was changed, stops
it at once, and what was unpacked so far is removed.


The encryption
--------------

One fixed set, the same in every sealed file, nothing to choose or
negotiate:

    password -> key   Argon2id: 32 MiB of memory, 2 passes, a random
                      16-byte salt per file (so the same password
                      gives a new key each time)
    encryption        XChaCha20-Poly1305, in chunks of 64 KiB

Argon2id makes each guess at the password cost 32 MiB and some time,
so guessing many passwords is slow even on graphics cards. It takes
about 0.05 s on a new computer and, estimated, 1-2 s on an old netbook
(an Eee PC 900); the rest runs at about disk speed.

Each chunk carries a mac: any changed byte, a chunk cut off, added,
swapped or left out is found, and said where. The last chunk is marked
as the last, so a file cut short at a chunk's end is found too.

The file is the salt and then the chunks: no header, no magic, no
version, no name of seal. It looks like random bytes from its first
byte to its last, so any name works, holiday.mp4 as well as baba.seal.
The directory's name, the names in it and their sizes are all inside
the encryption.

The code is taken from Monocypher 4.0.2 (public domain), only the
parts needed, rewritten in C89 into crypto.c. make test checks it
against libsodium and the Argon2 reference.

What goes in: names, contents, rwx permissions (0777) and symbolic
links (as links, never followed). What doesn't: owners, groups, times
(unsealed files get the time of unsealing), setuid bits, hard links
(each name gets its own copy). A device, pipe or socket in the
directory stops seal before it changes anything.

unseal never writes outside the directory it unpacks: names with ..,
/ at the start, or a directory part that is a link are refused, so
even a file made by someone else can't reach your other files.


-f: going on anyway
-------------------

A wrong password and a damaged file look the same at the first chunk:
unseal says "wrong password, or it is not a sealed file". With -f it
unpacks what it can anyway: a damaged chunk is decrypted without its
check, which gives back everything else and garbage in the damaged
64 KiB. It says each place, keeps the sealed file and exits with 1.
With a really wrong password nothing can be read, -f or not.


What it can't do
----------------

- Removing is not wiping. When seal removes DIR, the old contents can
  stay in the free blocks of the disk until something writes over
  them; on an SSD, overwriting doesn't even reach the same blocks.
  Disk encryption covers that while the computer is off; while it is
  on, root (or a program with access to the raw disk) could find them.
  Best: make secret files inside an unsealed directory in RAM from the
  start, so they are never on the disk unencrypted.
- The name fools a look, not an expert. holiday.mp4 won't play (a
  player says it is broken), but anyone who looks finds random bytes,
  not a video, and its size says roughly how much is inside.
- The password is typed where programs can see it. In X, every program
  of yours can read the keyboard, so a keylogger would get it. For the
  most secret ones, seal and unseal on a text console (Ctrl+Alt+F2),
  where X programs can't listen.
- While a directory is unsealed in RAM, it is a normal directory: open
  to every program of yours, until you seal it again. tmpfs can go to
  swap when memory runs low: keep swap encrypted too (or have none).
- seal keeps the password and the key out of swap and core dumps, and
  stops other programs of yours from reading its memory, but a program
  running as root can.
- Forget the password and the files are gone: there is no way back.
  Keep a copy of the important ones elsewhere.


Build and install
-----------------

Plain C (C89), only libc, static. install.sh builds it into
~/.system/bin, with unseal as a link to seal (no root needed):

    ~/dotfiles/install.sh seal

By hand:

    make
    make test          the crypto against references, then seal itself
    make PREFIX=$HOME/.system install

Files: seal.c (the commands, the password, RAM), pack.c (the archive,
its chunks, checking, unpacking), crypto.c (Argon2id,
XChaCha20-Poly1305, from Monocypher), test/vectors.c (the crypto
against libsodium and the Argon2 reference), test/run.sh (the tests).

crypto.c is by Loup Vaillant, Michael Savage and Fabio Scotoni
(Monocypher: public domain under CC0, with a 2-clause BSD licence as a
fallback; the licence is at the top of crypto.c). The untouched
Monocypher is in archive/monocypher.
